WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:00.920
Is Google

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:03.100
doing creepy things to us?

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:05.500
A new documentary says yes.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.960
Google crosses the creepy line

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:08.780
every day.

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:10.640
Before I explain the creepy line,

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:12.720
let’s review how we got here.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.320
Allison, can you explain what internet is?

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:16.906
Just a few decades ago,

00:00:16.906 --> 00:00:18.240
a time when people my age

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:20.320
didn’t understand computers.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.640
Mark Zuckerberg, who was a student at Harvard,

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:25.460
decided he wanted to create a social media platform

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:28.120
basically to meet girls and to make friends.

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:29.140
We were college students, right?

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:29.640
Yeah.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:30.520
And we were just building stuff

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:31.660
because we thought it was cool.

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:33.520
Larry Page and Sergey Brin

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:35.340
decide they wanted to create

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.600
the ultimate search engine for the internet.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.020
They had a better index

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:41.120
than other indexes that were around.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:43.360
Theirs was not the first search engine.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:45.280
It was just leaps and bounds ahead of services

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.210
like Yahoo, Alta Vista, and quickly became

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:49.620
the clear winner in the space,

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.560
just from a technology basis.

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:54.350
Google put answers to your question on one page,

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:55.280
and ranked them.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:56.980
It spiders around the internet

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:58.980
taking pictures of webpages

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:00.340
and analyzing the links between

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:01.580
the webpages,

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:02.880
and using that to

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.840
make some assumptions about relevance.

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:06.480
Now, they had to figure out,

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:07.800
how do we make money off this?

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:11.220
All you do is track people's searches.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:12.715
Your search history

00:01:12.715 --> 00:01:14.760
is very, very informative,

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.540
and that's going to tell someone immediately

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:18.540
whether you're a Republican or a Democrat,

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:22.380
whether you like one breakfast cereal versus another.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:24.920
Then they could sell you to advertisers.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.340
So they could hook up the umbrella makers

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:28.780
with people searching for umbrellas,

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:32.440
basically send you targeted ads.

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:33.900
That is where Google

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.500
gets more than 90% of its revenue.

00:01:36.509 --> 00:01:39.070
They don't sell you anything.

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:40.609
They sell you.

00:01:40.609 --> 00:01:41.480
Fine.

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:42.780
Because they know about me,

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.252
I get ads on my screen

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:46.820
that might actually appeal to me.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:48.520
So what’s the problem?

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:49.780
Well the filmmaker says,

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:51.180
this is the problem.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:54.920
They are constructing a profile of you.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:57.880
And that profile is real, it's detailed,

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:01.320
it's granular, and it never goes away.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:02.340
And what I didn’t know

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.220
until I watched The Creepy Line,

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:06.479
is how far Google goes

00:02:06.479 --> 00:02:08.220
to follow us on the web.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:09.620
They were getting a lot of information

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:12.000
from people using the search engine,

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.560
but if people went directly to a website,

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:15.480
uh-oh,

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:16.040
that's bad

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:18.920
because now Google doesn't know that.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:21.640
So they developed a browser

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:24.380
which is now the most widely used browser in the world,

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:25.110
Chrome.

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:26.780
By getting people to use Chrome,

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:29.710
they were able now to collect information about every

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:31.680
single website you visited,

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.800
whether or not you were using their search engine.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:35.960
But of course, even that's not enough, right?

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:37.580
Then Google wanted to know

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:38.800
what do people do

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:40.520
when they're not online?

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:42.020
As soon as you connect to the internet,

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:46.170
Android uploads to Google a complete history of where

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:47.880
you've been that day.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:48.640
So what?

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.320
They're giving me information.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:52.240
They are giving you information but

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:55.360
to the extent that somebody can do something for you,

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:57.160
they can do something to you.

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:59.210
Peter Schweizer wrote The Creepy Line.

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:00.830
I love these services.

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:03.060
You're seeing all the positive, good side of it.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:05.520
Yeah! Your movie's all about the bad side.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:07.440
That's why people hate the media.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:08.920
The question comes down to,

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:11.560
are they abusing their power?

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:13.660
And I think you can make a very important, powerful,

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:15.880
compelling case that they are.

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:18.670
His documentary suggests regulation.

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:20.540
Media companies are regulated,

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:21.760
newspapers are regulated,

00:03:21.764 --> 00:03:22.910
telecommunications providers

00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:23.560
are regulated.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:27.280
But regulation often makes things worse for consumers,

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:29.340
and politicians are too clueless

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:30.580
to do it well.

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:33.080
Watch Senator Hatch embarrass himself.

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:34.760
How do you sustain a business model

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:39.260
in which users don't pay for your service?

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:41.100
Senator, we run ads.

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:42.560
You want regulation?

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:44.380
That's going to make it better?

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:46.160
I think one of the ways you deal with

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:48.322
Google's market concentration,

00:03:48.322 --> 00:03:51.200
and its massive control of search

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:53.240
is put it under the same shackles

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:54.940
that other media companies do.

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:56.840
Shackles aren't good.

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:58.540
But government has the guns.

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:00.220
Government can always step in

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:01.230
Right.

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:01.820
at some point,

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:05.300
if the people find Google just doing evil.

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:06.360
Yeah.

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:07.819
But to start it now?

00:04:07.819 --> 00:04:09.171
I would rather say,

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:11.320
here are the ground rules

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:14.380
that other media companies have to subscribe to.

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:16.560
Google should be put in the same category.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.040
Now I don’t presume to know what,

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:18.780
if anything,

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:21.340
ought to be done to make sure Facebook and Google

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:23.280
don’t harm the world.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:25.760
But the documentary does make a compelling case

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:28.840
that these giant companies do creepy things.

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:30.920
There's what I call the creepy line,

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:33.420
and the Google policy about a lot of these things

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:35.670
is to get right up to the creepy line but not cross it.

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:37.620
It's an interesting word, creepy,

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:38.140
right,

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:40.360
because it's a word that connotes horror.

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:41.457
I don't think

00:04:41.457 --> 00:04:44.240
the typical, ethical person says,

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.630
I'm going to push right up to the line of creepy

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:48.260
and stop there.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:50.060
Because creepy is really bad.

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:52.560
A creepy mugger is worse than a mugger.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:54.040
The mugger wants your money,

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:56.560
God only knows what the creepy mugger wants.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:58.060
It’s more than your money.

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:01.158
In my second and concluding video on The Creepy Line,

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:04.000
we look at how a few people in Silicon Valley

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:06.860
secretly shape what we think,

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:09.360
and maybe, who will govern us.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:15.620
I am running for President of the United States!

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:17.780
Last election Silicon Valley tried,

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:20.180
but failed to elect Hillary,

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:21.820
but next election?

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:23.220
The Creepy Line says:

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:26.020
If the major players in tech right now,

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:27.860
and that's mainly Google and Facebook,

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:28.830
banded together

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:30.920
and got behind the same candidate,

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:34.280
they could shift 10% of the vote in the United States

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:36.540
with no one knowing that they had done anything.

